\DOC ENSURES_SEQUENCE_TAC

\TYPE {ENSURES_SEQUENCE_TAC : term -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Splits an {ensures} goal at an intermediate program point by inserting a
user-supplied midpoint state predicate, yielding the conjunction of the two
sub-executions as a single new goal.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {n : term} is the program location of the cut point, given
as an address expression (or an offset form), and is used to build the
midpoint's program-counter condition. The second argument {q : term} is a state
predicate {\s. ...} describing what holds at that point; it serves as the
postcondition of the first segment and the precondition of the second. The
intermediate predicate {R} is built from {q} by conjoining the program-loading
condition carried over from the precondition {P} together with a
program-counter condition fixed by {n}; the same frame {C} is used in both
halves. Where {q} has leading existential variables {x1 ... xn} (with {n}
possibly zero), these appear as explicit existential quantifiers inside {R}.
{
          A ?- ensures model P Q C
   ====================================  ENSURES_SEQUENCE_TAC n q
   A ?- ensures model P R C /\ ensures model R Q C
}

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single transformation on {ensures} goals for one
machine model.

\ENDDOC
